---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/for/b2b-wholesale';
const title = 'Shopify SMS apps for B2B wholesale: buyer authority and reorder controls';
const desc = 'Compare conditional SMS app fits for wholesale accounts, with company-location identity, approval handoffs, reorder eligibility and contribution tests.';
const faqs = [{"question": "Can wholesale customers receive automated marketing?", "answer": "Yes, when the individual has suitable channel permission and the current company, location, role and message purpose pass eligibility checks. Wholesale status alone is neither permission nor a universal exclusion."}, {"question": "Does a saved cart prove purchasing authority?", "answer": "No. A buyer may prepare a cart while another person approves the quote, places the order or authorizes payment. Verify each authority separately and route unresolved cases to the account representative."}, {"question": "When should a reorder reminder stop?", "answer": "Retire the pending reminder when the matching order is placed, the request is withdrawn, permission is revoked, the contact changes role, or the account becomes ineligible. A later valid reorder cycle needs fresh eligibility and a new queue key."}, {"question": "How should a pilot measure results?", "answer": "Randomize comparable eligible company accounts, keep their locations and contacts in the same group, and compare contribution per assigned account over the same reorder window. Report orders, delivered recipients, opt-outs and support load with explicit denominators."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/for" class="link">All store types</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Shopify SMS apps for B2B wholesale</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl">Choose around the buyer, approver and account representative who own the next step. A reorder reminder can help an authorized buyer; an abandoned quote awaiting credit approval needs a different response.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">This is a conditional shortlist and a proposed operating plan, reviewed November 25, 2026. The fictional workloads below are tests to run, not merchant results. No supplier is ranked and no combination is required.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16 space-y-8">
    <article class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Shortlist by the job you can validate</h2>
      <p><strong>Postscript:</strong> a candidate for a team concentrating on permissioned SMS campaigns. Its current site advertises SMS list building and message creation, alongside additional channels. Test whether your actual integration supplies company-location and contact-role fields before allowing wholesale audiences into a campaign. An SMS feature list does not establish purchasing authority.</p>
      <p><strong>Klaviyo:</strong> a candidate when the team wants marketing automation and customer data alongside email and SMS. Its official platform describes these capabilities; validate the actual B2B identifiers, event freshness and approval-state filters available in your account. A profile or consumer checkout event alone is insufficient for a wholesale quote reminder.</p>
      <p>These are distinct starting points, not evidence of universal superiority or native wholesale approval support. Ask each supplier to demonstrate role changes, queued-message cancellation, suppression export, reply routing, destination coverage and total billing with your own test records. Keep your current platform if it meets these requirements at lower total operating cost.</p>
      <p>Sources: <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/">Postscript platform</a> and <a class="link" href="https://www.klaviyo.com/solutions/marketing-automation">Klaviyo automation</a>. Product descriptions establish advertised capabilities, not a dispatch guarantee. See <a class="link" href="/apps">the app directory</a> for further evaluation.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Resolve company, location and person before every send</h2>
      <p>Shopify distinguishes companies, company locations and individual customers. Locations can have different pricing, payment terms and contacts. Use stable company ID, location ID and person ID together, plus the recipient's verified channel address. A shared purchasing inbox or phone number must not merge unrelated companies or expose another location's contract.</p>
      <p>Keep the person's current role, authorized location scope, message purpose, channel permission source, timestamp and disclosure version. Employment, an account application, an imported trade-show list, a quote request or an old order does not by itself grant promotional SMS permission. Confirm applicable messaging requirements and recipient-local sending restrictions with qualified advice and the provider; do not treat a single time window as universal.</p>
      <p>Separately record who may request a quote, edit a cart, approve a quote, place an order and authorize payment. A marketing subscription cannot confer those rights. Recheck at dispatch, including role removal, location transfers, disputed numbers and revoked permission. Fail closed on missing identity or stale permission; send the case to a named representative rather than guessing.</p>
      <p>Primary model reference: <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/b2b/companies-and-customers">Shopify companies and customers</a>. The marketing integration must be checked independently; this model does not prove that every field reaches either supplier.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Choose the next step from account and order truth</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Access request:</strong> keep application status separate from marketing permission. After account approval, offer onboarding only for the recipient's allowed purpose and channel; approval retires the pending application reminder.</li>
        <li><strong>Quote or cart:</strong> check expiry, version, location, price list, minimum order quantity and stock. A quote needing rep review gets a human handoff. Do not send a consumer discount or imply a saved cart is an approved order.</li>
        <li><strong>Approval:</strong> notify only the authorized approver through a permitted route. A buyer's click cannot stand in for approval. Approval, rejection or withdrawal retires obsolete requests and any related buyer chase queue.</li>
        <li><strong>Credit and payment:</strong> check current payment terms and credit hold with the finance owner. Do not ask a purchasing assistant to pay a net-terms invoice or expose balances on a shared phone. Route exceptions to finance or the rep through authenticated account access.</li>
        <li><strong>Repeat order:</strong> require a completed qualifying prior order, a plausible consumption window, eligible products and current contract terms. Exclude open replacement orders, unresolved returns, discontinued stock and account holds. Authorized opted-in buyers can remain eligible for suitable automation.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Link to authenticated account context rather than embedding confidential contract prices, credit details or another buyer's cart in a text. A link must revalidate access; possession of a forwarded message must not authorize purchase or payment.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Fictional workloads to test before rollout</h2>
      <p>Every example here is a hypothetical proposed test, with no claimed lift or executed result. Start with a dry run and a small approved audience.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Arena Energy, gym supply:</strong> propose a flavor availability notice to eligible gym buyers; verify location stock and contract pack sizes rather than a consumer athlete promotion.</li>
        <li><strong>Driftwood Surf, regional dealers:</strong> propose a seasonal reorder window by location. Test recipient timezone and purchasing scope rather than inferring permission from weather interest.</li>
        <li><strong>Noir Collective, boutique wholesale:</strong> compare two proposed reminder cadences by company account; measure contribution and complaints instead of asserting a frequency reduction improved revenue.</li>
        <li><strong>SoleDrop, footwear distribution:</strong> test size-run MOQ and allocation expiry before an opted-in buyer gets access. A pending approver receives a separate approved task.</li>
        <li><strong>Festival Threads, venue supply:</strong> test a location-specific delivery cutoff with the rep; cancel when a matching purchase order arrives outside the storefront.</li>
        <li><strong>Vinyl Vault, record shops:</strong> propose pressing allocation reminders without assuming historical order value permits VIP texting. Test one allocation per eligible location.</li>
        <li><strong>Bloom &amp; Wick, gift retailers:</strong> propose holiday case-pack planning by email, then test whether a permitted SMS deadline adds contribution after freight and rep effort.</li>
        <li><strong>Peak Trail, outdoor dealers:</strong> test clearance eligibility against protected contract pricing and territory restrictions; escalate conflicts to the rep.</li>
        <li><strong>Sauce Lab, food service:</strong> test batch availability and case MOQ; retire the queue if inventory becomes insufficient before dispatch.</li>
        <li><strong>Ember Ceramics, gallery supply:</strong> test allocation across limited kiln output, with manual rep confirmation for split shipments rather than a claimed sell-through time.</li>
        <li><strong>Ridge Run Coffee, office supply:</strong> test reorder eligibility against standing orders so scheduled deliveries do not trigger duplicate reminders.</li>
        <li><strong>Cipher Audio, reseller network:</strong> propose launch education without a discount; test authorized territory, approver and price-list access before order links.</li>
        <li><strong>Patchwork Denim, retail partners:</strong> test catalog preferences against actual available quantities; never infer company buying authority from browsing.</li>
        <li><strong>Loom &amp; Frame, gallery dealers:</strong> test edition reservations and quote expiry; a withdrawn reservation must retire both email and SMS queues.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <article class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Retire queues, including retries and late events</h2>
      <p>Use a durable message key containing company, location, person, purpose and quote/order or reorder-cycle version. Store dispatch state and an audit trail. Retries reuse that key with bounded backoff; a timeout is an unknown delivery outcome, not permission to create a fresh send. Reconcile provider receipts before retrying an ambiguous request.</p>
      <p>Approval, withdrawal, rejection, matching purchase, revoked permission and resolved support cases retire their applicable pending queues in every connected sender. Do not blanket-exclude all future wholesale messages after one purchase: a new legitimate cycle needs fresh qualification. Use order IDs and line/location matching so an unrelated location's purchase does not cancel valid work.</p>
      <p>Check current source-of-truth state immediately before dispatch. A late cart event must not reopen a purchased or withdrawn quote; compare versions and timestamps, retain terminal tombstones and reconcile offline rep orders. If delivery cannot be recalled, record the incident and pause further related sends. Never promise exactly-once delivery from a local dedupe table alone.</p>
      <p>Assign a human owner for replies, HELP, disputed identity, credit exceptions and failed links. Define a response deadline and an escalation queue. Keep support access available after a marketing opt-out, through a permitted service route, without automatically resubscribing the person.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Measure accounts, orders and contribution separately</h2>
      <p>Randomize comparable eligible company accounts, keeping their locations and contacts together to reduce contamination. Balance reorder cadence, size and contract terms. Freeze an observation window long enough for the purchasing cycle; use the same window for treatment and holdout and record rep contacts in both groups.</p>
      <p>Proposed arithmetic only: 40 treatment accounts generate 12 qualifying orders and $6,000 net sales; 40 holdout accounts generate 10 and $5,000. Orders per assigned account are 12/40 = 0.30 and 10/40 = 0.25. These are order rates, not percentages of accounts purchasing: count distinct purchasing accounts separately. The net sales difference is $25 per assigned account, not proof that every attributed order was incremental.</p>
      <p>If treatment product cost, fulfillment, platform/carrier allocation and rep/support labor total $4,500, contribution is $1,500 or $37.50 per assigned account. If holdout costs total $3,900, contribution is $1,100 or $27.50 per assigned account: a hypothetical $10 difference. Net sales already deduct discounts and returns once; do not subtract them again. Include credit losses and payment fees where applicable, with a consistent policy in both groups.</p>
      <p>Report delivered unique recipients separately from assigned accounts. Opt-outs divided by delivered unique promotional recipients measures recipient reaction; order conversion uses eligible assigned accounts or an explicitly stated order denominator. Include all assigned accounts in the pilot analysis even if delivery fails. Report uncertainty and repeat-cycle retention before expanding.</p>
      <p>Obtain current written quotes for destinations, message segments, carrier charges, profile tiers, support, integration work and peak volume. Compare a single platform and any proposed combination using the same workload. See <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">cost planning</a>; no historical price here is a purchasing quote.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Launch, recovery and rollback</h2>
      <p>Before launch, test a buyer becoming an approver, a buyer leaving the company, duplicate webhooks, a rep-entered purchase, a credit hold, a STOP reply while queued and a late quote update after withdrawal. Verify correct audience, destination authorization, visible offer terms, quiet-time handling, dedupe and terminal exits with actual account fixtures.</p>
      <p>Name the marketing owner, account rep, finance approver and technical responder. Agree stop thresholds for wrong-recipient exposure, stale prices, duplicate messages, complaints and contribution loss. On a breach, pause entry and dispatch, retire unsafe queued work, preserve suppression and event history, and reconcile orders with the rep. Restore a previously verified configuration only after replaying the failing cases in dry run; never replay old promotional queues blindly.</p>
      <p>Export permission and suppression evidence before migration. Confirm the former sender is stopped before enabling the replacement. Keep an incident log and a staffed recovery path for customers who received an obsolete reminder. Supplier features cannot replace ownership of these controls.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
